# ML Platform Costs: Databricks AI vs. SageMaker vs. DIY

You proved the vibration model works in a notebook — 94% recall on
bearing failure prediction. Now it needs to run in production: weekly
retraining, real-time scoring, experiment tracking, drift monitoring.
Three paths. Very different price tags. Very different trade-offs.

## The bottom line

| Platform | Monthly | Annual |
|----------|---------|--------|
| **Databricks AI** | **{{db_ml_monthly}}** | **{{db_ml_annual}}** |
| **AWS SageMaker** | **{{sm_ml_monthly}}** | **{{sm_ml_annual}}** |
| **DIY (MLflow OSS + VMs)** | **{{diy_total_monthly}}** | **{{diy_total_annual}}** |
| DIY (infra only, no ops labor) | {{diy_ml_monthly}} | {{diy_ml_annual}} |

The DIY path looks cheapest until you account for the 10 hours/month
someone spends keeping 5 services alive. SageMaker wins on raw
infrastructure cost. Databricks wins if you're already on the
platform — MLflow tracking and Lakehouse Monitoring are included,
and your feature tables are already in Delta.

**The rest of this document shows how we got here.** Every assumption
is explained. Disagree with a number? Change it and rerun.

---

## The Wind Utility ML Workload

Before pricing anything, nail down what the workload actually is.

### The model

One primary model: bearing failure prediction from vibration and
temperature data. An Isolation Forest or gradient-boosted classifier
trained on 6 months of historical SCADA readings. Not a massive
neural network — this is tabular ML on structured sensor data.

### Training

Retrain weekly on the latest 6 months of data. The feature table
is precomputed rolling statistics (mean, std, kurtosis of vibration
over 1h/6h/24h windows) joined with temperature and power output.

feature_table_size = 50 GB
training_hours_per_run = 6
training_runs_per_week = 1

A single GPU node (NVIDIA A10G or T4) handles this. You don't need
a multi-node GPU cluster for tabular ML on 50 GB — that's a common
over-provisioning mistake. One node, 6 hours, done.

### Serving

Real-time scoring: each turbine gets a risk score once per hour.
"This bearing has a 73% chance of failure in 48 hours." The model
is small (a few MB serialized), inference is fast (sub-millisecond
per prediction), but the endpoint runs 24/7.

turbines = 500
predictions_per_hour = turbines
hours_per_month = 24 * 30

predictions_per_month = predictions_per_hour * hours_per_month

That's predictions_per_month predictions/month. Trivial volume —
a single CPU endpoint handles this with room to spare.

### Experiment tracking and monitoring

During active development: ~50 experiment runs/month as the data
science team tries different feature windows, contamination
thresholds, and model architectures. In steady-state production:
4 runs/month (the weekly retrains).

dev_runs_per_month = 50
prod_runs_per_month = 4

Model monitoring tracks prediction distribution over time and
flags drift — if the model starts predicting "high risk" for 40%
of turbines instead of the usual 5%, something changed (sensor
calibration? seasonal pattern? actual problem?).

---

## Databricks AI Costs

If you're already running the SCADA pipeline on Databricks (Modules
1–6), the ML platform is an incremental cost on top of existing
infrastructure. MLflow tracking is built into the workspace. Feature
tables live in Delta. That's the pitch — and it's a real advantage.

### Training compute

Interactive GPU cluster for model development and weekly retraining.
All-Purpose Compute at $0.40/DBU because data scientists need to
iterate in notebooks during development. A GPU-equipped cluster
(1 A10G node) consumes ~24 DBUs/hour.

db_dbu_rate = $0.40
db_train_dbus_per_hour = 24
db_train_hours_per_week = 6

db_train_weekly = db_dbu_rate * db_train_dbus_per_hour * db_train_hours_per_week
db_train_monthly = db_train_weekly * 4

In production you could switch weekly retrains to Jobs Compute at
$0.15/DBU and cut this by 60%. We use All-Purpose as the
conservative case where the team is still actively developing.

### Model Serving

A small CPU endpoint running 24/7 for real-time scoring. Model
Serving endpoints bill at roughly All-Purpose rates, ~4 DBUs/hour
for a small instance. This is sized for our 500 predictions/hour —
well within a single small endpoint's capacity.

db_serve_dbus_per_hour = 4
db_serve_monthly = db_dbu_rate * db_serve_dbus_per_hour * 24 * 30

### MLflow tracking

Included. No separate charge. Runs on the workspace's managed
infrastructure. This is one of Databricks' strongest selling
points for ML — you don't pay extra for experiment tracking,
model registry, or the tracking UI. Compare that to managing
your own MLflow server.

### Feature Store (online)

The feature table lives in Delta (offline store — already paid for
in your storage costs). For real-time serving, you need an online
store. Databricks Feature Serving backs to DynamoDB or Cosmos DB.

500 turbines x 1 lookup/hour x 730 hours/month = 365,000 reads.
At $0.25/million reads plus ~1 GB of storage, this is tiny:

db_feature_store_monthly = $25

### Lakehouse Monitoring

Included with the workspace. Tracks prediction distribution,
detects drift, generates alerts. No additional charge — another
bundled benefit of the platform.

### Cloud infrastructure

Databricks runs on your cloud account. The GPU instances, serving
VMs, and networking appear on your AWS/Azure bill separately.
Typically 50% on top of DBU spend for this type of workload:

db_cloud_infra = 50% of (db_train_monthly + db_serve_monthly)

### Databricks ML total

db_ml_monthly = db_train_monthly + db_serve_monthly + db_feature_store_monthly + db_cloud_infra
db_ml_annual = db_ml_monthly * 12

---

## AWS SageMaker Costs

SageMaker is a purpose-built ML platform. It doesn't share
infrastructure with your data pipeline — that's both a strength
(optimized for ML workflows) and a weakness (another platform
to manage, another bill to understand).

### Training

ml.g4dn.xlarge: 1 NVIDIA T4 GPU, 4 vCPUs, 16 GB RAM. Plenty for
tabular ML on 50 GB. $0.526/hour on-demand (source: [AWS SageMaker
pricing](https://aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/pricing/)).

sm_train_rate = $0.526
sm_train_hours_per_week = 6

sm_train_weekly = sm_train_rate * sm_train_hours_per_week
sm_train_monthly = sm_train_weekly * 4

You could use spot instances and cut this further, but SageMaker
managed spot has some quirks with checkpointing. On-demand is the
safe estimate.

### Serving

ml.t2.medium: 2 vCPUs, 4 GB RAM. $0.065/hour for a real-time
endpoint running 24/7. Overkill for 500 predictions/hour, but
it's the smallest real-time endpoint available.

sm_serve_rate = $0.065
sm_serve_monthly = sm_serve_rate * 24 * 30

A serverless inference endpoint could be cheaper at this volume
(you pay per invocation), but serverless endpoints have cold start
latency that may not work for alerting use cases.

### Experiment tracking

SageMaker Experiments is included — no additional charge. The UI is
less polished than MLflow's, but it tracks parameters, metrics, and
artifacts. If you're already in the AWS ecosystem, it works.

### Feature Store

SageMaker Feature Store: $0.06 per 1,000 read requests for the
online store, plus storage. At our volume (365K reads/month), the
compute cost is negligible. Storage and base fees bring it to ~$15:

sm_feature_store_monthly = $15

### Model monitoring

SageMaker Model Monitor runs a processing job on a schedule to
check for data drift and prediction quality. For a single model
with weekly checks:

sm_monitor_monthly = $50

### SageMaker ML total

sm_ml_monthly = sm_train_monthly + sm_serve_monthly + sm_feature_store_monthly + sm_monitor_monthly
sm_ml_annual = sm_ml_monthly * 12

---

## DIY: MLflow OSS + Cloud VMs

The scrappy path. Open-source MLflow, spot GPU instances for
training, a Flask endpoint for serving. Maximum control, minimum
vendor lock-in, maximum operational burden.

### Training

GPU spot instance (g4dn.xlarge) at ~$0.20/hour — roughly 60% off
on-demand. Spot is fine for training because you can checkpoint and
resume. The risk is interruption, but for a 6-hour weekly job,
you'll typically complete without issues.

diy_train_rate = $0.20
diy_train_hours_per_week = 6

diy_train_weekly = diy_train_rate * diy_train_hours_per_week
diy_train_monthly = diy_train_weekly * 4

### Serving

Flask or FastAPI behind a load balancer on a t3.medium ($0.042/hour).
Handles 500 predictions/hour trivially. You write the serving code,
the health checks, the autoscaling rules, and the deployment pipeline.

diy_serve_monthly = $0.042 * 24 * 30

### MLflow OSS server

Self-hosted on a small VM ($0.05/hour for a t3.small, 24/7). Stores
experiment metadata in Postgres, artifacts in S3. You manage the
server, the database, the backups, and the upgrades.

diy_mlflow_monthly = $0.05 * 24 * 30

### Feature Store

Managed Redis (ElastiCache) or Postgres (RDS) for online features.
A small managed instance runs ~$50/month:

diy_feature_store_monthly = $50

### Monitoring

Prometheus + Grafana for infrastructure metrics, plus either
Evidently AI (OSS, self-hosted) or a managed observability stack
for prediction monitoring. A small managed Grafana instance:

diy_monitor_monthly = $50

### DIY infrastructure total

diy_ml_monthly = diy_train_monthly + diy_serve_monthly + diy_mlflow_monthly + diy_feature_store_monthly + diy_monitor_monthly
diy_ml_annual = diy_ml_monthly * 12

### The hidden cost: engineering time

Here's where DIY gets expensive. You're running 5 services:
training pipeline, serving endpoint, MLflow server, feature store,
monitoring stack. Each one needs patching, scaling, debugging,
and on-call rotation.

Conservative estimate: 10 hours/month of ops work. That's ~2.5
hours/week — one MLflow upgrade, one serving endpoint restart, one
monitoring alert investigation, one training pipeline failure.

engineer_hourly_rate = $80
ops_hours_per_month = 10
diy_ops_monthly = engineer_hourly_rate * ops_hours_per_month

The $80/hour rate is a blended cost — not the engineer's salary,
but the loaded cost of their time (salary + benefits + overhead,
divided by productive hours). A $160K/year engineer at ~2,000
productive hours/year = $80/hour.

### DIY total (with ops labor)

diy_total_monthly = diy_ml_monthly + diy_ops_monthly
diy_total_annual = diy_total_monthly * 12

---

## The Hidden Cost: Time to First Model

Infrastructure cost is the ongoing bill. But there's a one-time
cost that's easy to ignore: how long before the vibration model
is actually running in production, scoring turbines, and catching
failures?

### Databricks

MLflow is integrated. Model Serving is a button click. Feature
tables are already in Delta from your SCADA pipeline. The data
scientist can go from notebook experiment to production endpoint
in **1–2 weeks**. Most of that time is model development, not
infrastructure.

db_time_to_prod = 2 weeks

### SageMaker

Steeper learning curve. SageMaker has its own concepts (training
jobs, endpoints, endpoint configurations, model packages) that
don't map 1:1 to what the team knows from MLflow. The data lives
in Databricks/Delta, so you need a data export pipeline to S3.
**3–4 weeks**, with most of the extra time on infrastructure setup
and data plumbing.

sm_time_to_prod = 4 weeks

### DIY

Before anyone touches a model, someone has to set up: MLflow
server, Postgres backend, S3 artifact store, serving infrastructure,
CI/CD for model deployment, monitoring, and alerting. **4–8 weeks**
of infrastructure work before the data scientist can even start
iterating in a tracked environment.

diy_time_to_prod = 8 weeks

### Opportunity cost

Every week without a production model is a week without
automated bearing failure alerts. At 2 missed failures/year
($500K each), that's roughly:

missed_failure_cost = $1000000
weeks_per_year = 52
delay_cost_per_week = missed_failure_cost / weeks_per_year

The delay cost is real: delay_cost_per_week per week of delayed
deployment. Databricks' 6-week advantage over DIY is worth
roughly 6 * delay_cost_per_week in avoided risk — about
{{6 * delay_cost_per_week}}.

---

## When Each Platform Wins

### Databricks AI wins when:

- You're already running your data pipeline on Databricks (Modules
  1–6). The incremental cost of ML is small — MLflow tracking and
  Lakehouse Monitoring are free, your features are already in Delta.
- Governance matters. Unity Catalog tracks which model version was
  trained on which data, who approved the promotion to production,
  and what the prediction accuracy was at each stage. NERC auditors
  love this.
- You have fewer than ~10 models. Databricks Model Serving is
  simple but not as feature-rich as SageMaker for complex ML ops.

### SageMaker wins when:

- You're AWS-native and your data is already in S3. No reason to
  add a Databricks dependency just for ML.
- You need advanced ML ops: A/B testing between model versions,
  multi-model endpoints, automatic scaling based on traffic
  patterns, shadow deployments.
- You're running 10+ models in production. SageMaker's ML ops
  tooling (Pipelines, Model Monitor, endpoint auto-scaling) is
  more mature for large-scale ML operations.

### DIY wins when:

- Cost is the dominant constraint and you have ops skills on the
  team. The infrastructure bill is 2–3x cheaper.
- You have 1–2 simple models with infrequent retraining. The ops
  burden scales with the number of services, not the number of
  predictions.
- Vendor lock-in is unacceptable. MLflow OSS runs anywhere. Your
  training code is plain Python. Nothing ties you to a cloud
  provider.

### The honest take

For the wind utility — already on Databricks, one primary model,
NERC compliance requirements, a 2-person data science team that
should be building models and not managing infrastructure — **Databricks
is the right call**. The infrastructure cost is higher than DIY,
but the time-to-production advantage and the governance story
justify it.

If this were a startup with 3 engineers, no compliance requirements,
and one simple model? DIY with MLflow OSS every time.

